        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          How to Apply a Paint-On Waterproofing Sealer: Step-by-Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          The published PSI rating on any of these products only means something if the application is done correctly. Skipping surface preparation is the single most common reason a waterproofing sealer fails within the first year, regardless of how good the product itself is. Here is the process that gets a coating to perform at its rated pressure.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 1: Strip and Etch the Bare Masonry</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Cementitious and epoxy coatings need an open, bare surface to bond into — they will not adhere reliably over existing paint, old sealer, or efflorescence. Remove any loose material and existing coatings with a wire brush, scraper, or chemical stripper as needed. Then etch the surface with a masonry etching solution or diluted muriatic acid to open the concrete's pores and remove residual laitance. Rinse thoroughly and allow the wall to dry completely — most coatings require a fully dry substrate, and moisture trapped beneath the film is a leading cause of blistering and premature failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 2: Patch Cracks and Holes Before Coating</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Fill any visible cracks, tie holes, or honeycombing with hydraulic cement, which expands slightly as it sets and can be applied even against light active moisture. Let patches cure fully per the manufacturer's instructions before applying the sealer over them. Remember that rigid cementitious and epoxy coatings will not bridge an active moving crack on their own — if a crack continues to open and close seasonally, either address it with a flexible elastomeric coating or patch it with a product designed for movement before applying a rigid topcoat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 3: Apply Two Full Coats at the Rated Thickness</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Published PSI ratings assume a specific dry film thickness, almost always achieved through two full coats rather than one thick one. Work the first coat into the pores of the masonry with a stiff masonry brush, paying extra attention to mortar joints on block walls where water most commonly enters. Allow the manufacturer's specified cure time between coats — typically 24 hours for cementitious products, as little as 4 hours for thinner acrylic formulas. Apply the second coat with a roller for a more even finish, and resist the temptation to skip it to save time; a single coat rarely reaches the film thickness the PSI rating depends on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 4: Cure Fully Before Testing</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Most waterproofing coatings need 5 to 7 days of full cure time before they reach their rated performance, even though they may feel dry to the touch within hours. Avoid exposing the wall to standing water or heavy humidity during this window. Once fully cured, the simplest field test is a visual check after the next significant rain — if the treated wall stays dry while an untreated comparison area shows dampness, the coating is doing its job. If dampness reappears within a season, revisit surface preparation before assuming the product itself failed.
          </p>
        </div>

