            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does the PSI rating on a basement waterproofing sealer actually mean?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The PSI rating measures how much hydrostatic pressure — the force of water-saturated soil pushing against your foundation wall — the cured coating can resist before water is forced through it. One PSI is roughly equivalent to a 2.3-foot column of water. A typical residential basement experiences 2 to 5 PSI of pressure even during heavy, sustained rain, so a coating rated to 10 or 15 PSI carries a substantial safety margin for normal conditions. A basement with a documented high water table or a history of standing floor water may exceed what any paint-on product is rated to handle, which is a signal to consider drainage rather than a coating.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a paint-on waterproofing sealer replace a French drain or sump pump?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              No. Paint-on sealers are designed to stop moisture from migrating through the wall itself — condensation, dampness, and pressure up to their rated PSI. They are not designed to manage water that is actively reaching the basement floor, pooling at the cove joint after every storm, or coming from a high water table. If water appears on the floor rather than just on the walls, that is a hydrostatic drainage problem that a coating cannot solve regardless of its PSI rating, and it calls for an interior drain and sump pump system instead. See our full guide to <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems">basement waterproofing systems</a> for that comparison.
            </p>
