            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Nearly every paint-on basement waterproofing sealer requires two full coats to reach its rated dry film thickness and published PSI performance. A single coat, even a thick one, typically leaves pinholes and thin spots that compromise the pressure rating. Cementitious products like DRYLOK and RadonSeal need roughly 24 hours between coats; thinner acrylic products like Rust-Oleum Watertite can often be recoated in about four hours. Trowel-applied products like Thoroseal may achieve adequate thickness in a single heavy application, but two coats are still the safer standard for full pressure resistance.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will waterproofing paint stick to basement walls that are already painted or previously sealed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Cementitious waterproofing coatings like DRYLOK and RadonSeal require bare, uncoated, mechanically or chemically etched masonry to bond correctly — they will not adhere reliably over existing paint, old sealer, or efflorescence, and any prior coating must be stripped first. Epoxy-modified coatings like Sika WaterGuard are more tolerant and can sometimes bond over an existing coating after thorough mechanical abrasion, but manufacturer guidance should always be checked. When in doubt, strip the wall back to bare masonry — it is the single most common reason a new coating fails prematurely.
